Taking this up commits the household to a few concrete behaviours, not to agreeing with my diagnosis. Maintaining a single shared written record after each agreement, plus explicit definitions of done for recurring tasks — that is the load-bearing part, and it only works if all three treat it as a memory aid rather than a dossier; the record itself can become ammunition if used to score points, which is a real risk I flag rather than solve. Setting and protecting a short weekly check-in, and the inviter specifically shortening conversations after asking the avoiding friend what would help — the advice about avoidance depends on that follow-through. Tolerating approximate fairness is a scope choice, not a finding; if the underlying grievance is about respect or money rather than tasks, this system will paper over it and my recommendations should be revised. The one-month trial is a proposed test, and I am proposing it fallibly: persistent avoidance or escalation despite the structure would count against my claim that this is mainly a coordination problem, not confirm it. If one flatmate refuses even the trial, only the unilateral parts remain available to the asker — confirming agreements in writing, stating their own standards, attending to tone — and I cannot claim those alone resolve a three-person dispute. Unresolved: what to do when the avoiding friend also avoids the meta-conversation, which this account does not cover.